  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSaturday Night/Sunday System: Shortwave energy will dive into\nKansas Saturday night with low pressure across Oklahoma.\nInitially we will be dealing with some dry air however latest\nREFS guidance depicts that a band or bands of rain will develop\nalong and behind the front late Saturday night into Sunday\nmorning. The current structure and evolution of this system\nfeatures the highest rain chances generally north of Highway 60.\nMean total rainfall amounts from the REFS is generally between\n0.05-0.25in with the highest amounts north of Interstate 44.\nMean MU CAPE values are generally less than 250j/kg and limited\nto south central Missouri. Therefore not expecting many\nthunderstorms with this system. \n\nAs the surface low lifts through the area on Sunday morning, a\ntight pressure gradient will develop with increasing wind gusts.\nLatest NBM and RRFS have high chances (50-70%) for wind gusts \nover 30mph with some potential for wind gusts to reach 40mph in \nlocalized areas. Clouds will be slow to clear and expect a \nrather chilly/brisk Sunday with temps staying steady in the 40s \nbehind the front. \n\nWarming Temps Next Week: A more zonal to slightly southwesterly\nflow pattern will develop for next week. Warmer air in the low\nlevels should allow for highs to slowly climb a few degrees each\nday with 60s possible as early as Tuesday but more likely\nWednesday and Thursday.\n\nRain Chances Late Week: Ensembles then suggest that a more\nactive pattern could emerge by late next week as shortwave\nenergy moves out of the southwest by Thursday. NBM already has \nfairly high precip chances (40-60%) for this time period.\nMachine learning algorithms also hint at some thunderstorm\npotential as well during this timeframe.",
